  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 255 AM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nEarly This Morning through Today...\n\nMuch of today will be quiet with upper ridging still the main\ninfluence across central Indiana. Warm advection will bring\nin hot and humid air from the southwest, with highs in the\nlower and middle 90s common across the area. With dewpoints\nrising into the upper 60s to lower 70s, afternoon heat\nindices will be in the upper 90s to near 100 in some locations.\n\nWarm mid levels will help keep convection at bay for most areas.\nHowever, some influence from upper energy moving around the ridge \nas well as from a baroclinic zone to the northwest could generate\nsome convection in portions of the western and northern forecast\narea late this afternoon. Will have some mainly chance PoPs there.\n\nGiven the expected instability from the hot and humid conditions,\ncannot rule out an isolated severe storm with damaging winds.\nLocally heavy rain will also be a threat.\n\nTonight...\n\nThe baroclinic zone will sag into the northern forecast area \ntonight. Increasing 850mb winds, additional upper energy, and \npotential boundaries from earlier convection will all aid in\nproducing some mainly scattered convection tonight. \n\nSimilar conditions to late this afternoon will persist into the\nevening, keeping the threat for an isolated severe storm around. \nDiminishing instability should end the threat overnight. High\nmoisture content of the atmosphere will keep a threat for locally\nheavy rain around as well.\n\nWednesday...\n\nThe upper ridge will get flattened some on Wednesday, making\nupper flow more zonal. This will keep the baroclinic zone in the \narea, keeping the threat for showers and thunderstorms around. \nUncertainties in where this zone will end up along and in sky \ncover/rain coverage leads to decent uncertainty in high \ntemperatures. High temperatures could range from the middle 80s\nto middle 90s from north to south.\n\nThursday through Saturday...\n\nThe upper ridge will gradually shift southwest. At the surface,\na front will meander across the region. These will keep the threat\nfor showers and thunderstorms around at times, but timing of the \nhighest PoPs remains uncertain. For now will keep PoPs in the \nchance category or lower.\n\nDepending on where the front is, high temperatures will vary across\nthe area from the lower 80s to the lower 90s, with the potential for\nmiddle to upper 90s in the far southwest depending on how far\nsouthwest the ridge gets.\n\nSunday and beyond...\n\nThe surface front will gradually move south of the area. This will\nallow cooler air to gradually filter in. Scattered convection will\naccompany the front, with chances lowering as the front exits. \nHighs will cool into the 70s for Monday with lows in the 50s.",
